William Kahan, “Branch Cuts for Complex Elementary Functions, or Much Ado About Nothing's Sign Bit,” in The State of the Art in Numerical Analysis, edited by A. Iserles and M. J. D. Powell, first collection edition, Clarendon Press, 1987. Robert M. Corless and David J. Jeffrey, “The Unwinding Number,” article commonly cited with year 1996, edition not applicable. Both records are unverified; the latter's precise title/year attribution and venue especially remain to be checked, and no venue is asserted here. These references motivate explicit branch conventions and correction terms when manipulating complex logarithms. The algebraic route uses root relations and product constraints, so it does not require an implemented unwinding-number function or a floating branch convention.

